---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';

const title = 'How to Charge a Lead-Acid Battery: Stages, Voltages and the Two Mistakes';
const description = 'Lead-acid charging has three stages — bulk, absorption and float — at specific voltages. Overcharging causes gassing and water loss; undercharging causes sulfation. Here is what each stage does and the right voltages for 12V flooded and AGM batteries.';
const canonical = '/lead-acid/lead-acid-charging/';

const articleJsonLd = {
  '@type': 'Article',
  '@id': `https://worldbatteryhub.com${canonical}#article`,
  headline: title,
  url: `https://worldbatteryhub.com${canonical}`,
  inLanguage: 'en',
  about: 'Lead-acid battery charging',
  articleSection: 'Lead-Acid Battery Knowledge',
  dateModified: '2026-09-15',
  isPartOf: { '@id': 'https://worldbatteryhub.com/#website' },
};
---

<BaseLayout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  canonical={canonical}
  ogType="article"
  jsonLd={[articleJsonLd]}
  author="World Battery Hub"
  dateModified="2026-09-15"
>
  <section class="section">
    <div class="container">
      <p class="byline">Lead-Acid Knowledge · Last reviewed 2026-09-15</p>
      <h1>How to Charge a Lead-Acid Battery</h1>
      <p class="lead">
        Lead-acid charging is a three-stage process — bulk, absorption, float — at specific voltages.
        The two classic mistakes are overcharging (gassing and water loss) and undercharging (sulfation).
      </p>

      <div class="takeaways">
        <strong>Direct answer:</strong> A 12V lead-acid battery is charged first at constant current
        (bulk), then held at a constant voltage around <strong>14.4V</strong> (absorption) until full, then dropped to a
        <strong>float of about 13.5–13.8V</strong> to hold it without overcharging. AGM batteries use slightly lower
        voltages; equalization (≈15.5V) is a periodic flooded-only step, not for AGM or gel.
      </div>

      <h2>The three stages</h2>
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Stage</th><th>What happens</th><th>Typical 12V voltage</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Bulk</strong></td>
            <td>Constant current pushes charge in until the voltage rises to the absorption limit</td>
            <td>rises to ~14.4V</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Absorption</strong></td>
            <td>Constant voltage finishes the charge; current tapers down as the battery fills</td>
            <td>~14.4–14.8V (flooded) / ~14.2–14.4V (AGM)</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Float</strong></td>
            <td>Low maintenance voltage holds the battery full without overcharging</td>
            <td>~13.5–13.8V (flooded) / ~13.4–13.6V (AGM)</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>

      <h2>The two mistakes</h2>
      <p>Lead-acid chemistry punishes both extremes:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Overcharging</strong> — holding the battery too high too long electrolyzes the water into hydrogen
          and oxygen (gassing), losing electrolyte and corroding the positive grid. In sealed/maintenance-free or AGM
          batteries the water cannot be topped up, so overcharging is especially damaging.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Undercharging</strong> — if the battery never reaches full, lead sulfate crystals left on the plates
          harden (sulfation) and no longer convert back, permanently reducing capacity and cranking power.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Voltage depends on chemistry and temperature</h2>
      <p>
        The exact voltages depend on the battery type and its temperature. Flooded, AGM and gel each have their own
        recommended limits, and chargers compensate voltage with temperature — lower voltage when hot, higher when cold.
        Charging a battery at the wrong type's voltage is a common cause of early failure.
      </p>

      <h2>Charging and CCA</h2>
      <p>
        Charging is also where cranking performance is preserved. Sulfation from chronic undercharging raises internal
        resistance, which is exactly what lowers cold cranking amps (CCA) over time — see
        <a href="/lead-acid/cca/what-is-cca/">what CCA is</a> and
        <a href="/technology/internal-resistance/">internal resistance</a>.
      </p>
      <h2>How long does charging take?</h2>
      <p>
        Bulk charging follows the division — capacity to replace ÷ charger current — but the finish is slower: the
        charger switches to constant voltage and the current tapers, so the final 20% takes about as long as the
        first 80% (see <a href="/lead-acid/charging-time-calculation/">the charging-time calculation</a> with
        worked examples).
      </p>

      <h2>How to choose a charger</h2>
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Check</th><th>Rule</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td><strong>Size</strong></td><td>~10–20% of the Ah rating — a 100 Ah battery pairs with a 10–20 A charger</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>Type match</strong></td><td>The profile must match the battery — flooded, AGM, gel or lithium</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>Voltage limits</strong></td><td>Within the battery's absorption and float limits for its type</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>


      <h2>Our Read</h2>
      <div class="takeaways">
        <p>
          <strong>In my view:</strong> Most lead-acid "sudden death" is really slow undercharging — a battery left below
          full for weeks sulfates, and no jump-start fixes that.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>What drives this:</strong> Sulfation is silent and cumulative; it shows up as a weak cold start long after the
          damage was done. The cheap fix is keeping the battery charged, which is why the float stage exists.
        </p>
        <p class="muted"><em>My view as an editor, not a purchasing guarantee.</em></p>
      </div>

      <h2>Sources</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>Battery Council International (BCI) — lead-acid charging and maintenance guidance.</li>
        <li>Manufacturer charging guides (Yuasa, Canadian Energy, Varta) — flooded vs AGM voltage limits.</li>
        <li>Cross-verified with charger documentation on bulk/absorption/float and temperature compensation.</li>
      </ul>

      <p class="muted">Last reviewed: 2026-09-15</p>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
